{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses because the preference for 'better-corroborated' theories implicitly relies on the very inductive assumption Popper rejected: that past survival under severe tests is a reliable indicator of future performance or verisimilitude. By advising scientists to 'prefer' one theory over another for action, the target smuggles in a probabilistic expectation (that the preferred theory will not fail immediately), which contradicts the claim that corroboration is merely a historical report card with no predictive power.","premise":"Popper explicitly denied that corroboration implies any increase in probability or likelihood of future success.","successor_question":"If corroboration offers no inductive weight, what rational basis exists for preferring Theory A over Theory B for critical applications other than an implicit belief that past success predicts future reliability?","target":"0eb89cbfbccaadff57f25b9a9bb07839f78ebb406a59ab900e75dd3a734364e3"}]}